is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: leaf_router_0_0
diagnosis_text:
- Affected component: The access interface on the leaf switch/router serving pc_0_0 is down (leaf_router_0_0’s eth2). This is a data-plane/interface issue, not a host NIC or cabling fault on pc_0_0.
- Mechanism: pc_0_0 (10.0.0.2) can’t ARP its default gateway 10.0.0.1, so all traffic off-subnet fails. On pc_0_0, eth0 is UP/LOWER_UP with an IP and default route, but there are zero RX packets and ARP/ping to 10.0.0.1 time out. On leaf_router_0_0, the facing port eth2 (10.0.0.1/24) is DOWN and ethtool reports “Link detected: no,” so L2 adjacency never forms and ARP never completes.
- Localization: leaf_router_0_0, interface eth2 toward pc_0_0. Evidence: ip addr shows eth2 state DOWN; ifconfig shows eth2 flags 4098 <BROADCAST,MULTICAST>; ethtool reports no link; pc_0_0 cannot ARP 10.0.0.1 and shows no RX.
- Root cause: The leaf access port to pc_0_0 (leaf_router_0_0 eth2) is administratively or operationally down, isolating the host.
- Corrective fix: Bring leaf_router_0_0 eth2 up (e.g., ip link set dev eth2 up) or restart the device’s networking to restore the access port. If you prefer a simple operational step, restart the device leaf_router_0_0; after restart, verify eth2 is UP and ARP/neigh entries form. FRR/BGP control plane appears healthy (leaf has BGP route to 10.0.1.0/24), so only the access interface needs recovery.

Recommended action: Restart device leaf_router_0_0 (or explicitly bring up interface eth2).
=== END ===